Subject: [PATCH] Steady audio RTP timestamps from a sample-clock (fix OPUS/WebRTC jitter)
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable).
Stacks on: 0001-0007. Applies with git apply -p1 / patch -p1 AFTER them. (Touches globals.hpp
and AudioWorker.cpp in regions disjoint from 0007's audio_stream/process_frame edits.)

Audio RTP timestamps were stamped from gettimeofday() at delivery (IMPDeviceSource::
deliverFrame, audio branch), so per-packet RTP timestamp deltas carried capture/scheduling
jitter (measured on the wire: 1252..2164 ticks instead of a constant 1920 for fixed 40ms
OPUS frames @48k clock). WebRTC's NetEQ is intolerant of irregular audio timestamps -> small
stutters. ffmpeg re-encode (clean sample-clock) is smooth -> the payload was fine, only the
timing was wrong.

Fix: stamp audio from a monotonic SAMPLE counter that advances by each frame's exact audio
duration, so the per-packet RTP delta is constant. Re-anchor ONLY on a large FORWARD wallclock
drift (>250ms = a genuine gap: stall / standby-resume / session restart / forward NTP step);
negative drift (drained queue bursts, backward NTP step) is absorbed by the steady counter and
does NOT re-anchor -- preserving the original author's NTP/stale-frame robustness without
reintroducing per-packet jitter. A 5s-throttled LOG_INFO fires on re-anchor so the field can
confirm it only triggers on real gaps/reconnects, never per-packet.

ALL arithmetic stays in the CAPTURE clock (sample_rate); live555's SimpleRTPSink converts the
presentation-time delta to the RTP clock (48000 for OPUS), so a 40ms frame -> +1920 ticks and a
20ms frame -> +960 automatically. IMPDeviceSource never references 48000. RTCP Sender Reports
(re-derived from fPresentationTime every ~5s by live555) keep A/V in sync under slow clock skew.

Also fixes a pre-existing AAC + force_stereo data-flow bug exposed by deriving the timestamp
from the sample count: the AudioReframer always outputs 1024 MONO samples (2048 bytes), but the
run-loop sized its buffer by outChnCnt and mislabeled the frame as 2x its real length, so
process_frame double-dupped the audio AND the per-channel frame_samples (the timestamp step)
came out 2x too large. The buffer is now sized to the reframer's true mono output. No effect on
OPUS (no reframer), mono AAC (unchanged), or any non-stereo-forced config.

- globals.hpp: AudioFrame gains `uint32_t samples` (per-channel PCM count, default 0).
- AudioWorker.cpp: process_audio_frame sets af.samples = frame_samples (after the clamp); the
  reframer buffer is sized 1024*sizeof(uint16_t) (mono), not *outChnCnt.
- IMPDeviceSource.hpp: audio anchor state (audioAnchorUs / audioSamplesSinceAnchor / audioAnchorRate);
  single-instance because USE_AUDIO_STREAM_REPLICATOR shares one upstream source across clients.
- IMPDeviceSource.cpp: the audio branch of deliverFrame replaced with the sample-clock + drift-gated
  re-anchor + strict-monotonic backstop. Video branch untouched.

